 {:source/id :host/e-gov
  :host/name "laws.e-gov.go.jp"
  :host/kind :statute-api
  :host/api-base "https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/api/2/laws?law_id="
  :host/listing-base "https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/api/2/laws"
  :host/live-repeal-token "None"
  :host/repeal-tokens ["Repeal" "Expire" "LossOfEffectiveness"]
  :host/enforced-token "CurrentEnforced"
  :host/revision-tokens ["CurrentEnforced" "Repeal" "PreviousEnforced" "UnEnforced"]
  :host/uncontrolled-revision-tokens ["UnEnforced"]
  :host/repeal-fields-in "revision_info"
  :host/identity-fields-in "law_info"
  :host/fabricated-id-status 200
  :host/corpus-size 9551
  :source/verify :verify/statute-host
  :host/note
  "The listing endpoint answers a fabricated law id with 200 and total_count
   0, so no check here reads its status. Identity is total_count = 1 plus an
   exact law_id match. The fields are split across two objects and the split
   is not intuitive: law_num and promulgation_date are in law_info, while
   law_title, repeal_status and current_revision_status are in revision_info.
   Read from the wrong one they come back nil, and nil is one keystroke from
   the live not-repealed token None while meaning the opposite. Presence of
   the field is therefore asserted separately from its value. Being in force
   is repeal_status AND current_revision_status, because neither alone is
   sufficient, and remain_in_force is not a third opinion -- counted over the
   whole corpus it is true on 14 laws and NONE of them is unrepealed. Token
   sets are counted over all 9550 rows, not assumed. Three of the four
   revision tokens are held live by controls; UnEnforced deliberately is NOT,
   and that is named in :host/uncontrolled-revision-tokens rather than left
   silent -- an UnEnforced law becomes CurrentEnforced on its own enforcement
   date, so pinning one as a control would schedule a false failure. The
   verifier prints it as uncontrolled on every run instead of reporting
   four-of-four coverage."
  :host/measured "2026-08-27"}

